00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:13.260
with everything we cut for time at CuriosityStream.com/Wendover.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.690
Supermarkets are a marvel obscured by banality.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:22.750
Nearly everyone in the developed world uses
them regularly, so we have no basis of comparison—we

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.150
just don’t know a world without supermarkets.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:30.460
We don’t know how it was a century ago,
when grocery stores took the form of small

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.140
storefronts, found every few blocks throughout
towns and cities.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.570
You’d hand the clerk a list indicating you
wanted, among other things, apples.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:43.420
They’d then gather you apples—assuming
they were in season and in stock.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.910
Nowadays, however, you are confronted by an
aisle rivaling the size of those historic

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:53.660
grocery stores, displaying a tantalizing,
sprawling selection of Honeycrips, Fujis,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:01:01.030
Granny Smiths, Galas, Braeburns, and more—all
the varieties, always in stock, all year long.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:06.890
They sit next to pineapples from Costa Rica,
avocados from Mexico, and mangos from Brazil.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.119
Further on, there’s beef from an entirely
different hemisphere, and fish that was alive

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:13.790
in an ocean thousands of miles away just days
ago.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:19.829
A century ago, you could certainly buy a jar
of peanut butter, but now, you can buy regular

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:24.600
peanut butter, or chunky peanut butter, or
smooth peanut butter, or organic peanut butter—and

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.310
it doesn’t stop there.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:31.279
You can get the chunky Jif brand, or the chunky
Smucker’s brand, or the Skippy brand, the

00:01:31.279 --> 00:01:34.409
store brand, or one of so many more.

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:39.709
You have dozens upon dozens of choices of
peanut butter, dozens of choices of other

00:01:39.709 --> 00:01:45.349
nut butter, dozens of choices of other sandwich
spreads, and all of that is just one part

00:01:45.349 --> 00:01:47.259
of one aisle.

00:01:47.259 --> 00:01:53.149
Put together, the stability and variety of
choice in modern supermarkets is incredible.

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:59.799
In big cities and small towns alike, American
supermarkets offer an average of some 30,000

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:05.609
different product choices which stay in-stock
about 92% of the time.

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:10.220
While the concept of the modern supermarket
first appeared in the US, it's since proliferated

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.280
across the entire developed world.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:19.110
The fact that this has become not only normal,
but expected globally is the greatest indicator

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:24.790
of the robustness of the modern global supply
chain, but that’s not to say that it’s

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:25.790
easy.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:30.510
For consumers, it’s simple—we can get
everything we want, anytime, from a single

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:35.450
store—but the complexity behind that is
truly stunning.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:41.560
This is a typical American supermarket—a
City Market brand store in Glenwood Springs,

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:42.560
Colorado.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.060
While this brand is small, it’s part of
the larger King Soopers brand, which itself

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.450
is part of Kroger’s—the largest American
supermarket company.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:56.280
The entire American supermarket landscape
is incredibly consolidated, with the top four

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.700
companies holding 45% of the market, which
certainly has massively negative consequences,

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:08.460
but this market concentration has led to the
huge scale and complexity of our current food

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:09.890
supply chain.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:15.020
Even independent grocers now tend to rely
on gigantic cooperatives to amass buying power

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:21.600
and supply their shelves, so industry-wide,
scale and complexity is the norm.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.710
Now, supermarkets like this are involved with
a perpetual balancing act.

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:31.650
Keeping items in stock is of paramount financial
concern—research into the matter has found

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:36.350
that on the third instance of a desired item
being out of stock, consumers will go to an

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:39.700
alternate store 70% of the time.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.160
Being located just five minutes from a Walmart,
this store can’t afford to go out of stock

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.390
of a single item, push a customer to a competitor,
and lose out on thousands of dollars in annual

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:52.560
sales because they decide they like Walmart
more.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.700
Therefore, the task is to keep everything
in stock as much as possible, while having

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.230
as little extra product as possible.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:05.440
So, the way this Glenwood Springs City Market,
along with essentially any grocery store,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.910
keeps those 30,000 products continuously on
its shelves is simple, at least on the surface.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:16.959
You see, every item in a supermarket is labeled
with a barcode—usually that code is standardized

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.439
industry-wide, except with some white-label
products.

00:04:20.439 --> 00:04:25.300
When products arrive at the store, they are
checked in to its inventory management software.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:29.610
From there, it’s simple math—as products
are checked out and paid for, they’re subtracted

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:34.900
from the inventory count, and as that count
gets low, the store knows it’s time to reorder.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:40.270
It’s a straightforward concept—except
when you actually implement it in the real

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.270
world.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:47.069
There’s more than one way a product can
leave a store—it can get stolen, go bad,

00:04:47.069 --> 00:04:49.080
get damaged, or more.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:55.130
That means there’s always a slight disparity
between how many items there are on paper,

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.290
and in reality.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:59.930
Inventory management software can account
for some of that, assuming employees feed

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:04.840
it accurate data, but stores also conduct
a manual count every month or two to determine

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:06.560
the actual disparity.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:11.550
This is most important for financial reporting
reasons—the retailer can’t know how profitable

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:16.520
it actually is until it knows how much product
it lost—but it can also be used to tell

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.759
the inventory management software how much
it’s typically off, and correct for that

00:05:20.759 --> 00:05:24.099
in the future to make sure re-orders happen
on time.

00:05:24.099 --> 00:05:26.470
Then, there are other factors.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:30.870
For example, if a supermarket runs a sale
on a given item, that product will likely

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:35.550
sell more, so inventory management software
needs to account for that in its ordering

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:40.150
process to make sure that it correspondently
ramps inventory up.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:45.620
Then, incoming unseasonably warm weather could
mean that barbecue charcoal sales, for example,

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:50.409
are about to increase, while hot chocolate
sales will decrease, so more complex inventory

00:05:50.409 --> 00:05:55.020
management software can account for external
factors like these and make ordering decisions

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.570
based upon them.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:00.070
Now, some products are simple to keep in stock.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:04.680
Oreos, for example, have a long shelf live
and come from a massive manufacturer with

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.870
multiple production facilities spread out
across the world.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:12.110
There’s plenty of slack and flexibility
in that system.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.759
That’s not the case with all foods.

00:06:14.759 --> 00:06:17.339
Take, for example, grapes.

00:06:17.339 --> 00:06:20.199
Table grapes are quite difficult to keep in
stock.

00:06:20.199 --> 00:06:25.080
If there’s a sudden surge in demand for
grapes, you can’t just order more from the

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:29.800
factory—their global inventory levels are
essentially decided years before as growers

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:33.050
decide whether to add or subtract vines from
their vineyards.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:36.220
What’s more, grapes are highly seasonal.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:41.139
They don’t ripen off the vine, so they have
to be picked exactly when they’re best for

00:06:41.139 --> 00:06:47.030
eating—effectively meaning growers have
a one or two week window to get a given vineyard

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:48.039
harvested.

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:53.830
Of course, grapes are found in grocery stores
for far more than two weeks a year—in fact

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.020
they’re almost always available.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:02.900
What makes that possible is a massive production
cycle spanning across the entire western hemisphere.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:08.090
Now, the easy part of the year for the Glenwood
Springs City Market to get its grapes is late

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:09.090
summer.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.680
That’s because California’s central valley,
where the vast majority of the country’s

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:17.499
grapes are grown, has its natural harvest
season between mid-August and late-September.

00:07:17.499 --> 00:07:23.539
That’s extended earlier into July by “ultra-early
season” varieties of grapes, such as Sharada

00:07:23.539 --> 00:07:29.029
UA, and then later into November by late-season
varieties such as the “autumn king.”

00:07:29.029 --> 00:07:33.550
Therefore, there’s about a four-month window
when the entire country’s grape supply is

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:36.300
largely fulfilled by California.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:40.969
Once that ends, though, things get trickier,
but the industry has learned to take advantage

00:07:40.969 --> 00:07:42.789
of global climate patterns.

00:07:42.789 --> 00:07:49.199
Peru, thanks to its fairly equatorial climate,
begins its harvest just when California’s

00:07:49.199 --> 00:07:54.400
ends, early-December, and saturates the market
until mid-February, when Chile, with its more

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.229
seasonal climate, takes over.

00:07:56.229 --> 00:08:01.139
They essentially act as the southern-hemisphere
equivalent of California, also using early

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:06.039
and late season varieties to stretch their
massive harvest all the way until May.

00:08:06.039 --> 00:08:11.289
Next, California’s Imperial and Coachella
valleys, as well as various locations in Mexico,

00:08:11.289 --> 00:08:13.199
fire-up their harvests.

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:17.800
These areas have year-round warm weather that
allows for a year-round growing season, but

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:21.919
time their vineyards specifically to line
up with this gap in the market until mid-July,

00:08:21.919 --> 00:08:27.479
when California’s more seasonal Central
Valley begins its harvest—starting the cycle

00:08:27.479 --> 00:08:28.990
once again.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:33.539
While the overall path a given food product
takes varies dramatically, the one constant

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:38.789
is that every item that ends up on the Glenwood
Springs City Market’s shelves comes via

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.820
here—the distribution center.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:47.020
Now, how distribution centers work on the
surface level is fairly standard—they bring

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:51.450
in pallets of a single product, break them
down to the box level, and create pallets

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:55.160
with smaller quantities of everything a store
needs.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.690
How each distribution center accomplishes
that, however, differs.

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:04.920
Kroger’s Aurora facility, operated by a
company called Windigo Logistics, uses a fairly

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.330
high degree of automation.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:12.150
Trucks arrive from all across North America,
delivering pallets worth of product from Kroger’s

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:13.630
different vendors.

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:18.130
For example, a truck might arrive from Nabisco’s
plant in Chicago carrying pallets worth of

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:19.380
Oreos.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:24.370
These pallets are immediately placed on an
induction conveyor belt, where a scanner determines

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:25.820
what they actually are.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:29.940
With that information, the warehousing software
determines whether it thinks it will need

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:33.300
more Oreos in its system in the next three
days, or later.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:38.990
If the answer’s later, the pallets just
make a quick stop at a pallet exchanger machine,

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:43.870
which swaps each onto a fresh storage pallet,
before the conveyor belts take them to an

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:47.680
automated longer-term storage system in a
separate part of the warehouse.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.910
If one pallet worth of Oreos is needed sooner,
though, it will go to a de-palletizing machine,

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:57.680
which breaks them down to the box level, each
of which carries dozens of units of the actual

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:58.680
product.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:02.910
Those boxes are injected into a network of
conveyor belts, which takes them to a machine

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:05.650
that places each onto an individual tray.

00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:10.410
That tray is then automatically stored somewhere
in a cavernous hall of shelving—essentially,

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.120
a massive vending machine.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:16.410
Sometime in the next three days, some King
Soopers or City Market store will place a

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:21.530
re-order, indicating that they need Oreos,
and so the warehousing software will automatically

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:26.330
remove some trays of Oreos from the shelving
and place them on another conveyor belt, which

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:30.640
will take them to a smaller automated shelving
system closer to the palletizing area, which

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:34.390
acts as interim storage, keeping items no
more than a few hours.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:39.550
Then, when it comes time to build an actual
pallet worth of product for a given store,

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:42.440
the trays are removed from the shelving in
a specific order.

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:47.010
You see, the system knows the composition
of the shelves of each of their stores, so

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:49.500
it knows what order to build a pallet in.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:53.980
They’ll put items for one side of a store
aisle at the bottom of a pallet, items for

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:58.430
the middle of that aisle in the middle, and
items for the other end at the top.

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:02.430
That way, the stockers in the store can break
down a pallet, and put things immediately

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.980
on the shelves in the most efficient manner.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:10.460
At the distribution center, this palletizing
process is still completely automated, and

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:14.910
in the end, the system will deliver completed
pallets to the humans responsible for loading

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:16.410
outbound trucks.

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:21.500
Finally, once that’s all completed, a truck
will take the three-hour journey west to Glenwood

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:27.190
Springs’ City Market, where an overnight
crew restocks the shelves with fresh product.

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:33.440
Now, even as automation is making significant
inroads, plenty of grocery distribution centers

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.950
still use a far more manual approach.

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:41.910
That process is essentially the same—pallets
are taken in, stored, and outbound pallets

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:45.880
are built with smaller quantities of each
product—but each step is just completed

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:48.170
by humans instead of machines.

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:53.480
However, both the automated and manual processes
are not perfect.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:58.960
Specifically, they present an issue for what’s
referred to as “slow moving inventory.”

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:03.940
You see, grocery distribution centers are
traditionally configured to work by the pallet

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:08.920
load—it just isn’t efficient to take in
a smaller amount—but sometimes, products

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.030
just aren’t that popular.

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:17.550
However, unpopular products are actually crucial
to the business strategies of modern supermarkets.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:22.360
The reason why they might have dozens upon
dozens of types of salsas is that some niche

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:27.320
brand on the bottom shelf is probably at least
one person’s favorite.

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:31.780
The City Market in Glenwood Springs might
carry that salsa, but the Walmart or Target

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:32.910
might not.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:37.111
Even if that one consumer can get everything
else they need at one of the competitors,

00:12:37.111 --> 00:12:41.090
they’re going to keep coming back to the
City Market because they know they can get

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:44.920
that one, niche product that they love.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:50.010
Supermarkets view niche, slow-moving products
as key differentiators, and key to customer

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:54.640
retention, so they’re certainly willing
to sell these products even at a loss.

00:12:54.640 --> 00:13:00.260
In fact, Walmart experimented with reducing
its product variety by 11%, but quickly reversed

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:05.900
course as it became clear that this ultimately
had a negative impact on their bottom line—even

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:08.770
if it simplified their distribution logistics.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:13.540
On the warehouse side, though, if each store
that sells that niche salsa only sells a dozen

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:18.240
of them per week, it’s going to take quite
a while for even the entire company to work

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.400
their way through a single pallet.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:25.080
In the distribution center, though, a single
pallet in the picking area takes the space

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:30.210
of a single pallet, regardless of whether
it’s used up in a day or a month.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:34.930
Therefore, every additional niche product
stocked increases the amount of storage space

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:39.190
needed, and increases the overall distance
that pickers have to travel to assemble an

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:43.240
average pallet—in the case of manual warehouses.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:47.970
To mitigate these effects, while still maintaining
their offerings of niche products, some supermarket

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:53.600
chains, including Kroger’s and H-E-B, have
set up entire, dedicated distribution systems

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:55.800
for slow-moving inventory.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:14:00.120
By setting up these facilities, they can ship
out less than box-load shipments of certain

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:04.910
products, and stock them without slowing down
the process for fast-moving inventory.

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:09.490
Of course, this process does still require
a certain amount of scale to work better than

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:14.100
the alternative—while Kroger’s has set
it up in densely-populated areas of the US

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:18.650
east-coast, it has yet to do so in the more
sparsely-populated rockies region home to

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:21.200
their King Soopers and City Market brands.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:26.480
There just isn’t enough overall demand in
the region to justify a dedicated setup.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:31.920
The pure scale at which the supermarket industry
operates is what has led to this level of

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:33.810
supply-chain complexity.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:38.880
When a single facility is responsible for
distributing a sizable portion of a state's

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:45.760
food supply, tiny, incremental efficiency
improvements can lead to huge savings for

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:46.760
companies.

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:51.510
While such commoditization and consolidation
of food supply is viewed as problematic by

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:57.440
many people, ultimately, the average consumer
just want a wide variety of food at a low-cost.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:01.480
That’s what this system provides.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:05.990
Any modern supply chain is molded into what
leads to commercial success, and as long as

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:10.540
people continue to view the convenience of
having 30,000 options of what to eat at a

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:16.550
moment’s notice as paramount, this is how
the developed world will continue to get their

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:17.550
food.

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:22.230
So, anyone who stayed to this point in the
video probably enjoys watching Wendover videos,

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:27.220
and would enjoy watching as much of them as
possible, but unfortunately, the YouTube algorithm

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:28.630
doesn’t feel the same.

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:34.440
For any channel, there’s a sweet spot of
video length—for us, it’s about 15 minutes—and

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:39.050
whenever we do anything longer than that,
the algorithm actually punishes us.

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:43.210
We always have to cut for time, but in order
to not feel like that work was for nothing,

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:48.180
we’ve started to upload extended cuts of
our videos, including this one, to Nebula—the

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:53.320
streaming site home to all your favorite educational
creators, founded by those same creators.

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:58.100
In addition, their unique business model allows
for big-budget projects, of which we’ve

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:01.170
made plenty across HAI and Wendover.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:05.230
Of course, the best way to get access to Nebula
